WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.501
Why is demonstration-free autonomous learning so hard?

00:00:03.581 --> 00:00:05.359
In the non-episodic setting,

00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:09.272
an untrained forward agent wanders off to arbitrary
states,

00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:14.909
so every new attempt starts from a wildly
different, often useless initial condition.

00:00:14.989 --> 00:00:17.628
That instability makes learning collapse.

00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:21.741
Prior work patched this in ways that reintroduce
human effort.

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:25.189
Some methods still ask for occasional manual resets.

00:00:25.269 --> 00:00:30.164
Others only succeed when the useful interactions
happen to occur by chance.

00:00:30.244 --> 00:00:34.276
And the two most directly comparable methods, VaPRL
and MEDAL,

00:00:34.356 --> 00:00:36.398
both lean on demonstration data,

00:00:36.478 --> 00:00:42.367
either to seed a subgoal curriculum or to define
what the backward agent should return to.

00:00:42.447 --> 00:00:48.535
The motivation here is to build an agent that
provides its own anchor and its own curriculum,

00:00:48.615 --> 00:00:51.520
using nothing but the experience it collects.
